  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Colours     Default (the game's own) plus: Enhanced, chosen on the menu, off
              by default.
  Enhanced    All 44 pictures painted with 13 fixed colours: Robbie and his
              cans in turquoise and blue, the pests in raspberry, pink, lemon
              and gold, leaves in three greens; a pixel left unpainted keeps
              the game's colour. A first pass. Off by default. A painted set,
              colourist not named.

  Pace        original

  Changes     1.0: figures as hardware sprites in their own colours over the
              game's own drawing
              1.1: Enhanced colours (painted, a first pass), two cheats, and
              the Next's DivMMC automap switched off at the start: NextZXOS
              leaves it on, and on current cores that could crash the game or
              spoil its drawing

  Note        Robbie, his spray cans, the spray and the pests are hardware
              sprites in their own colours, laid on the pixels the game still
              draws, so a pest no longer colours the leaves, the stalk or
              Robbie as it crosses them. The flower, the leaves and the lives
              are drawn once and stay as the game draws them. Enhanced paints
              the sprites. Proven on machine-played games of the first level
              and on every graphic through the game's own draw routine; later
              levels are not proven in play.

-- MENU ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   1  Infinite lives
   2  Immunity

  From an archive .pok for this game, each checked against this dump by
  reading the code it changes. Infinite lives also tested in machine play: the
  lives count never falls. Immunity is not played.

